%T A398719 12798232771390,887551898648022,69384410803391416,6062332162937402426,
%U A398719 587692420186427913478,62811360707965017126940,7360166308876374108794306,940930725608602464658510854
%N A398719 Number of even words of length 2n over the positive integers such that no proper nonempty prefix is even and the letters first appear in increasing order.
%C A398719 A word is called even if every letter occurring in it occurs an even number of times.
%C A398719 The condition that the letters first appear in increasing order means that the initial permutation of the word is of the form 12...k. Here the initial permutation is obtained by deleting the second and every subsequent occurrence of each letter.
%C A398719 Equivalently, a(n) counts set partitions of {1,...,2n} into blocks of even size such that, for every j < 2n, at least one block has an odd number of elements in {1,...,j}. The blocks are labeled in increasing order of their least elements.
%C A398719 To count these words, consider a prefix in which k distinct letters have appeared and exactly r of them currently have odd multiplicity. There are three possible types of next letter:
%C A398719 1. One of the r letters of odd multiplicity can be appended. Its multiplicity then becomes even, so the number of letters of odd multiplicity decreases from r to r-1.
%C A398719 2. One of the k-r previously occurring letters of even multiplicity can be appended. Its multiplicity then becomes odd, so the number of letters of odd multiplicity increases from r to r+1.
%C A398719 3. The next new letter k+1 can be appended. It occurs for the first time, so both the number of distinct letters and the number of letters of odd multiplicity increase by 1.
%C A398719 A prefix is even precisely when r=0. Therefore, for a word counted by a(n), the value r=0 is forbidden at every proper prefix. Immediately before the last letter is appended, exactly one letter must have odd multiplicity; appending that letter completes the even word.
%C A398719 Let F(t,k,r) be the number of admissible prefixes of length t using exactly k distinct letters, exactly r of which have odd multiplicity. The initial condition is F(1,1,1)=1.
%C A398719 For t >= 1 and r > 0, F(t+1,k,r) = (r+1)*F(t,k,r+1) + (k-r+1)*F(t,k,r-1) + F(t,k-1,r-1).
%C A398719 The three terms correspond respectively to appending a letter that previously had odd multiplicity, appending a previously occurring letter that had even multiplicity, and introducing the new letter k.
%C A398719 Finally, a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n} F(2n-1,k,1).
%H A398719 John Tyler Rascoe, <a href="/A398719/b398719.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..100</a>
%F A398719 a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n} F(2n-1,k,1), where F(1,1,1)=1 and F(t+1,k,r) = (r+1)*F(t,k,r+1) + (k-r+1)*F(t,k,r-1) + F(t,k-1,r-1), with F(t,k,r)=0 if t <= 0, k <= 0, r <= 0, or r > k.
%e A398719 For n = 2, we have the words 1212, 1221, so a(2) = 2.
%e A398719 For n = 3, the 14 words are 121112, 121121, 121323, 121332, 122212, 122221, 122313, 122331, 123123, 123132, 123213, 123231, 123312, 123321. Hence a(3) = 14.
%o A398719 (Python)
%o A398719 from functools import lru_cache
%o A398719 @lru_cache(maxsize=None)
%o A398719 def F(t,k,r):
%o A398719     if t<=0 or k<=0 or r<=0 or r>k: return 0
%o A398719     if t==1 and k==1 and r==1: return 1
%o A398719     return (r+1)*F(t-1,k,r+1) + (k-r+1)*F(t-1,k,r-1) + F(t-1,k-1,r-1)
%o A398719 def A398719(n):
%o A398719     if n<1: return 1
%o A398719     return sum(F(2*n-1,k,1) for k in range(1,n+1)) # _John Tyler Rascoe_, Aug 21 2026
%Y A398719 Cf. A005046, A124321, A156289.
